workers are busy reconstructing the  hector martinez building in the center  of saradena last month a car bomb  exploded destroying much of this block  dissidents of former fire rebels are  being blamed for the attack which killed  one person the buildings house human  rights groups social organizations and  unions  we were holding a security meeting  because of the way the conflict was  evolving with direct attacks against  social organizations and community  leaders  the farc dissidents are accusing us of  sympathizing with the eln and that's how  they justified attacking us  arauca an oil-rich region on the border  with venezuela has been mostly calm  since the signing of a peace deal  between the government and farc rebels  in 2016.  but violence returned as farc dissident  groups began to fight eln rebels for  control of drug trafficking and  contraband routes  since the beginning of the year more  than 60 people have been killed others  kidnapped or forcibly displaced  delgado sister of security guards  delgado killed in the explosion says  locals are terrified  this war is absurd and my brother is  just one of many victims everybody is  anxious because we don't know what will  happen tomorrow  even on a quiet day people can't sleep  can't rest well they fear leaving their  house everything has been affected  normally at 6 30 p.m the streets of the  center of saramena would be alive with  people shopping but since the conflict  started shops are closing early and  people are hurrying home fearing another  possible attack and on top of that the  mayor has imposed a curfew every night  starting at 9 pm  the government has increased its  military presence in the area  the violence has pushed more than a  dozen health workers to quit after  hospitals were also threatened by the  groups  catholic priest albert geraldo is  holding healing sessions to help workers  deal with trauma  he says part of the problem is that the  military is mainly protecting the oil  infrastructure in a region long  neglected by the government  it's a problem that goes back in history  the government has always prioritized  protecting military infrastructure  instead of the people the church has  asked the president to reopen the door  to a peace dialogue with the eln there  was supposed to be a meeting in  stockholm the government didn't go we  are also trying to talk to then  dissidents but we are on our own  for hundreds have left others like sonia  lopez promised to rebuild and continue  their work for the people even if it  might cost them their life alessandra  al jazeera
 

